<TopicPage slug="subjects/geography/advanced/population-and-migration-geography">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Population geography maps <strong>who lives where, how fast that changes, and who moves —
    and why</strong>. Two reversals define the moment: global population growth is slowing
    faster than anyone forecast, and the world's people are ageing while Africa stays young.
    Migration — always contested, always constant — connects the two halves of the map.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the transition, the reversal, and the flows between them" widget="equationStep" config={popCfg} />

  <h2>The debates worth knowing</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The Malthus rematch:</strong> 1968's Population Bomb predicted famine; the Green Revolution deferred it. The rematch now concerns climate and water rather than calories — but the question "is growth sustainable?" has never left.</li>
    <li><strong>Ageing societies:</strong> fewer workers per retiree means pension arithmetic fails quietly. Japan's response (robots, later retirement, immigration) is the global dress rehearsal.</li>
    <li><strong>Climate migration:</strong> the World Bank projects 200+ million internal climate migrants by 2050 — mostly rural-to-urban within countries, not the international flows headlines fear. Geography of vulnerability, mapped.</li>
    <li><strong>Migration economics:</strong> migrants send remittances, fill labour gaps and send skills home (brain circulation vs brain drain). The evidence is clearer than the politics allows.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> UN World Population Prospects (2024); IOM World Migration Report; Lee, "The Demographic Transition" (2003).</p>
</TopicPage>
